# UserPrompt Service Implementation Checklist

Status: Draft implementation contract derived from
`Docs/user-prompt-service-proposal.md`. The proposal is authoritative. If this
checklist conflicts with it, correct the checklist before continuing.

No production implementation begins until the proposal's version 1 contract is
approved. Once execution begins, checked items are completion claims and require
the evidence specified by their phase gate.

## Governing contract

- [ ] Implement the typed `UserPromptServiceProvider` and one process-wide
      `UserPrompt` runtime exactly as defined by the proposal.
- [ ] Support only the action-specific `prompt_map_location(options)` operation
      in contract major 1.
- [ ] Preserve the completed provider, namespace, runtime-generation,
      immutability, error, reload, and packaging conventions.
- [ ] Preserve one authoritative input-hook chain and one authoritative
      completed-render chain; do not add a fullscreen screen or parallel hooks.
- [ ] Treat the proposal's public API, errors, input table, callback ordering,
      context-menu exclusion, namespace attribution, tooltip suppression,
      native indicator ownership, lifecycle behavior, and acceptance criteria
      as mandatory.
- [ ] Do not implement any rejected alternative or non-goal from the proposal.
- [ ] Preserve unrelated worktree and index state. Do not stash or commit while
      executing this checklist.
- [ ] Start every production module with `--@ module=true`, load project modules
      with `reqscript()`, and register reload-managed modules in dependency
      order.
- [ ] Add LuaDoc for every new or modified class and method. Define every closed
      discriminator set as an immutable numeric enum.
- [ ] Keep focused unit, complete unit, syntax, build, package, focused live,
      complete live, rendered-screen, installed-runtime, reload, and cleanup
      evidence separate.
- [ ] Perform a follow-up implementation review at every phase gate before
      marking that phase complete.

## Phase 0 - Approve the contract and establish the baseline

- [ ] Record explicit approval of
      `Docs/user-prompt-service-proposal.md`, including the version 1 API,
      `SERVICE_BUSY`, tooltip suppression, lifecycle callbacks, and namespace
      border attribution. Record the approval date, baseline Git HEAD, and the
      approved proposal's SHA-256 content hash in this checklist's execution
      evidence; completing this or any later phase must not modify the proposal.
- [ ] Record the repository branch, revision, worktree/index state, package
      version, module registry order, and configured installed-runtime
      resolution before production edits.
- [ ] Identify and preserve every pre-existing modified or untracked file,
      including the proposal and this checklist.
- [ ] Run `./tools/Run-UnitTests.ps1` and record complete baseline successes,
      failures, errors, and pending counts.
- [ ] Run `./tools/Check-UnitTestNaming.ps1` and record the baseline result.
- [ ] Run `./tools/Check-LuaSyntax.ps1 -IncludeTests` and record production and
      test/support file counts.
- [ ] Run `./tools/Build.ps1 -LiveReload:$false` and record the offline build
      result without making an installed-runtime claim.
- [ ] Run `./tools/Publish.ps1`, which invokes the package verifier, then rerun
      `./tools/VerifyPackage.ps1 -SourceDir src -ZipPath <published-zip>
      -ExpandedPath <published-directory>` against the emitted paths; inspect
      expanded and archive outputs independently.
- [ ] Run one focused tooltip DwarfSpec baseline and one focused context-menu
      DwarfSpec baseline with terminal results and `cleanup_confirmed`.
- [ ] Record current native, overlay, and Lua-screen surface support and any
      baseline limitation that affects UserPrompt acceptance.
- [ ] Verify the proposal traceability table in this checklist maps every
      acceptance criterion, non-goal, and rejected alternative to at least one
      implementation phase and planned evidence row; correct omissions before
      production work.

### Phase 0 completion gate

- [ ] Proposal approval is recorded in this checklist with its approval date,
      baseline Git HEAD, and content hash, and no unresolved contract choice is
      hidden in an implementation task.
- [ ] Baseline failures and unrelated worktree changes are explicitly recorded
      and are not attributed to UserPrompt.
- [ ] The traceability table covers every proposal requirement and rejected
      alternative.
- [ ] The follow-up review confirms production work has not begun and the
      recorded baseline is sufficient to distinguish regressions.

## Phase 1 - Characterize existing seams and map the change

- [ ] Map the current provider flow from `dwarfuicore/services` through provider,
      acquisition, immutable API, adapter, service record, process state,
      identity, reload, registry, and package ownership.
- [ ] Map the current context-menu input path through native
      `plugins.overlay.feed_viewscreen_widgets`, Lua-screen `onInput`, root
      reconciliation, handler dispatch, predecessor delegation, and reload
      adoption.
- [ ] Map every internal context-menu opening and closing path, including the
      service method needed to close an existing session without selecting an
      entry.
- [ ] Map the tooltip intent, presenter, renderer, completed-render hooks,
      selected transport, redraw, motion, resize, and reload-adoption path.
- [ ] Map current pointer and map sampling and prove where
      `dfhack.gui.getMousePos()` can return nil.
- [ ] Revalidate the sibling DwarfDirect evidence read-only: its input-owning
      surface runs before competing dwarfmode handlers and consumes handled
      input by returning true. Record why its fullscreen screen, validation,
      and targeting state remain rejected here.
- [ ] Map the identity and API validation code and record why the existing
      map-registration handle registry and hard-coded API handle validation are
      insufficient or sufficient for `MapLocationPrompt` handles.
- [ ] Verify from the target DFHack source and installed bindings that
      `gui.paint_frame()` centers a supplied title and determine its exact cell
      capacity and clipping behavior.
- [ ] Verify the target DFHack representation and inactive sentinel for
      `df.global.game.main_interface.recenter_indicator_m` and prove direct
      assignment does not pan, recenter, change z-level, or alter follow state.
- [ ] Record the sibling DwarfUI mood-popover selection path and the underlying
      DFHack implementation that establish `recenter_indicator_m` as the native
      tile-selection indicator; do not edit either sibling checkout.
- [ ] Add characterization tests for context-menu pre-delegation order, exact
      predecessor arguments and returns, foreign wrappers, failure behavior,
      root reconciliation, and Core reload adoption.
- [ ] Add characterization tests for tooltip completed-render selection,
      single-owner dispatch, draw order, motion, resize, clamping, suppression
      feasibility, and Core reload adoption.
- [ ] Record a concrete sufficiency verdict for the current input and render
      seams. For each insufficient seam, name the smallest internal extension
      required; do not authorize unrelated shared refactoring.
- [ ] Produce the exact expected production, unit, DwarfSpec, documentation,
      registry, reload, build, and package file map.

### Phase 1 completion gate

- [ ] Characterization tests fail under representative changes to input order,
      predecessor preservation, completed-render owner selection, or reload
      adoption.
- [ ] The input/render sufficiency verdict is supported by current code and
      tests, not by the removed implementation plan.
- [ ] Every proposed shared-type change is tied to a specific version 1
      requirement that cannot be implemented safely through the current API.
- [ ] The follow-up review confirms the file map and smallest-change decisions
      cover the proposal without expanding consumer scope.

## Phase 2 - Establish prompt-first input ownership

- [ ] If Phase 1 proves the current single-handler input seam sufficient, add
      prompt-first arbitration through that owner without changing its public
      shape and record why no generalization was needed.
- [ ] If it is insufficient, generalize the existing context-menu input-hook
      owner into the smallest reload-safe internal dispatcher that can host
      UserPrompt first and context-menu opening second.
- [ ] Retain exactly one authoritative native trampoline and weakly owned
      Lua-screen trampolines; remove or reduce the old context-menu hook to one
      intentional adapter instead of leaving a parallel owner.
- [ ] Preserve native and Lua-screen root reconciliation, foreign outer-wrapper
      adoption, current-generation checks, and reload-safe inert trampolines.
- [ ] Dispatch consumers in deterministic private order, stop at the first
      `true`, and skip every later consumer and predecessor after handling.
- [ ] When every consumer returns false, invoke the predecessor exactly once
      with unchanged arguments and preserve all returns including trailing nils.
- [ ] Let the dispatcher identify an injected consumer's owned boundaries before
      protected dispatch so consumer failure still returns `true`; prove this
      infrastructure behavior with a stand-in consumer before UserPrompt state
      exists.
- [ ] Report protected-dispatch failure to the injected consumer owner without
      embedding UserPrompt lifecycle knowledge in the shared dispatcher. Defer
      actual prompt termination and diagnostics integration to Phases 7 and 8.
- [ ] Preserve the characterized failure policy for events not owned by the
      injected consumer.
- [ ] Prepare/reconcile the current map-capable input surface before prompt
      state can be published; expose a rollback-safe internal activation seam.
- [ ] Update process ownership, registry order, reload cleanup, package
      inventory, diagnostics, and focused tests for the selected design.

### Phase 2 completion gate

- [ ] Stand-in consumer tests prove priority order, exact boolean handling, exact
      predecessor preservation, owned-failure consumption, inactive
      transparency, failure notification, and no duplicate dispatch after
      construction or reload.
- [ ] Tests prove exactly one native input trampoline and no parallel live
      context-menu/UserPrompt hook owner.
- [ ] Existing focused context-menu input unit and live behavior remains green
      when no prompt is active.
- [ ] The follow-up review verifies every stand-in-owned failure remains consumed
      and every unowned path delegates according to the characterized contract;
      integrated prompt-owned failure proof remains in Phase 7.

## Phase 3 - Establish completed-render composition and tooltip suppression

- [ ] If Phase 1 proves the current render owner sufficient, add private support
      for an injected authoritative presentation intent and ordinary-tooltip
      suppression flag, and record why no generalization was needed.
- [ ] If it is insufficient, extend the current completed-render owner with the
      smallest internal compositor/presenter arbitration needed for tooltip and
      UserPrompt intents.
- [ ] Retain exactly one authoritative completed-render hook chain for native,
      overlay, and Lua-screen surfaces.
- [ ] Preserve current tooltip registration, target selection, selected intent,
      redraw, layout, renderer, and reload behavior while no prompt is active.
- [ ] Prove with injected stand-in intent that the owner can suppress only
      ordinary tooltip presentation while retaining tooltip registrations and
      selected intent unchanged. Defer binding this flag to active UserPrompt
      state until Phase 7.
- [ ] Route an injected authoritative presentation intent only through its
      selected supported root and do not transfer it to an unrelated root.
- [ ] Keep the authoritative-intent slot independent from tooltip registration
      and context-menu presentation state; defer the concrete prompt renderer to
      Phase 5.
- [ ] Define rollback-safe preparation and non-failing publication/retirement
      operations for the activation transaction.
- [ ] Preserve reload-safe trampoline adoption and one completed render per
      selected owner.
- [ ] Update registry, reload, package, diagnostics, and focused tests for the
      selected design.

### Phase 3 completion gate

- [ ] Existing tooltip unit and final-render DwarfSpec behavior is unchanged
      while no prompt is active.
- [ ] Stand-in intent tests prove the suppression capability hides only the
      ordinary tooltip window and that normal presentation resumes with retained
      intent when suppression clears.
- [ ] Tests prove one completed-render owner per surface with no duplicate or
      stale authoritative-intent rendering across Core reload.
- [ ] The follow-up review confirms no second tooltip backend, normal tooltip
      registration, fullscreen screen, or parallel render hook was introduced.

## Phase 4 - Implement prompt values, handles, and state transitions

- [ ] Add `USER_PROMPT` to the existing immutable numeric `ServiceKind` enum and
      update every exhaustive validator, count, prefix map, diagnostic, and test.
- [ ] Add `SERVICE_BUSY` to the immutable numeric error contract with the exact
      stable token and extend count/mapping tests without changing existing
      category values or meanings.
- [ ] Add immutable/copying value objects for
      `MapLocationPromptOptions` and the internal request snapshot.
- [ ] Validate that options contain only `title`, `message`, `on_select`, and
      optional `on_cancel`; require their proposal-defined types and accept
      empty strings and embedded newlines.
- [ ] Copy the API-bound validated namespace into the request snapshot. Do not
      accept a namespace display field or infer attribution from prompt text.
- [ ] Add a dedicated immutable `MapLocationPrompt` handle registry backed by
      an allocated composite identity unless Phase 1 proves a smaller safe use
      of an existing generic handle facility.
- [ ] Preserve handle validation precedence: malformed is `INVALID_ARGUMENT`,
      recognized old generation is `STALE_HANDLE`, another current domain is
      `FOREIGN_HANDLE`, and a terminal same-domain handle returns false.
- [ ] Add immutable numeric internal enums for every closed prompt state and
      terminal-cause set.
- [ ] Implement one process-wide authoritative pending-request slot with atomic
      busy rejection before menu, input, render, or indicator mutation.
- [ ] Implement one idempotent state-machine terminal decision shared by the
      modeled completion, cancellation, cleanup, failure, and reload causes.
- [ ] Mark the old handle inactive and clear pending state before invoking
      ordered injected cleanup ports for input, render, tooltip suppression,
      indicator ownership, and invalidation. At this phase, use stand-ins rather
      than claiming those later adapters are integrated.
- [ ] Invoke at most one mutually exclusive callback after every injected cleanup
      port has run; contain callback failure and record private diagnostics.
- [ ] Retain request callbacks until termination. Dropping an API or handle must
      not cancel an active prompt.
- [ ] Add diagnostics for active identity/namespace, state, terminal cause and
      counts, callback failures, and current generation without exposing
      callback functions.

### Phase 4 completion gate

- [ ] Focused tests cover exact option validation/copying, immutable values,
      handle identity and error precedence, busy rejection, terminal same-domain
      behavior, garbage collection, and namespace isolation.
- [ ] State-model tests use injected cleanup stand-ins to cover every terminal
      cause, repeated terminal requests, callback failure, mutual exclusion, and
      state-before-cleanup-before-callback ordering. They do not count as
      integrated input, render, indicator, lifecycle, or live evidence.
- [ ] Tests prove callback-visible positions are detached values and consumer
      mutation cannot alter stored options or ownership.
- [ ] The follow-up review confirms one process-wide prompt, no queue or
      replacement path, exact error tokens, and no spoofable namespace label.

## Phase 5 - Implement the prompt renderer and native indicator adapter

- [ ] Add an input-transparent prompt renderer with distinct interior title and
      message regions, wrapping, background/frame styling, preferred placement
      below the pointer, and viewport clamping.
- [ ] Pass the validated owning namespace as the title argument to
      `gui.paint_frame()` so it appears centered in the top border.
- [ ] Keep prompt title and namespace attribution as separate renderer fields
      and visual roles.
- [ ] Size the frame to the greater of wrapped content requirements and
      `#namespace + 6` cells when the viewport permits.
- [ ] When the namespace does not fit, pre-truncate it to the available label
      cells: use the longest fitting prefix plus ASCII `...` when at least four
      cells are available, otherwise use the fitting prefix without an ellipsis.
- [ ] Center the already-truncated namespace and retain the complete namespace
      in request and handle state.
- [ ] Recompute layout on content, pointer, and viewport changes and invalidate
      old/new screen cells so motion and disappearance do not leave artifacts.
- [ ] Hide the prompt window and native indicator without terminating when no
      screen-pointer coordinate is available; restore presentation when pointer
      coordinates return.
- [ ] Add an injected native-indicator adapter over
      `df.global.game.main_interface.recenter_indicator_m`.
- [ ] Snapshot a detached pre-prompt indicator value before the first write and
      retain the exact last value written by the prompt.
- [ ] Assign the current map hover directly while available and the verified
      inactive representation while unavailable; never call
      `dfhack.gui.revealInDwarfmodeMap()` or `renderMapOverlay()`.
- [ ] Before every later write, compare the native value with the prompt's last
      write. On mismatch, record external takeover, relinquish ownership for the
      rest of the prompt, stop writing, and skip restoration.
- [ ] On termination, restore the detached snapshot only if the prompt still
      owns the current native value.
- [ ] Keep visual hover sampling separate from the authoritative input-event
      completion sample.

### Phase 5 completion gate

- [ ] Renderer tests cover distinct namespace/title/message content, full and
      empty strings, exact copied text, embedded-newline preservation, wrapping,
      preferred below-pointer placement, every-edge clamping, motion, resize,
      pointer absence, invalidation, and input transparency.
- [ ] Namespace tests cover exact centering, `#namespace + 6` sizing, all
      truncation branches, narrow viewports, full-state retention, and absence
      of any consumer override.
- [ ] Indicator tests cover initial snapshot, hover motion, off-map hiding,
      owned restore, external takeover before each write after the first and
      before restoration, and modeled adapter termination invocations without
      pan, z-level, or follow-state changes. These are not integrated lifecycle
      claims.
- [ ] The follow-up review confirms prompt completion never reads the indicator
      cache and the renderer is not registered as a normal tooltip.

## Phase 6 - Add the public provider and assemble the runtime

- [ ] Add the private version 1 adapter using the existing provider acquisition
      pattern and validate all public arguments before lazy initialization.
- [ ] Add immutable `UserPromptServiceProvider` and
      `UserPromptServiceApi` types with `get_contract_version()`,
      `get_namespace()`, `prompt_map_location()`, `cancel()`, `is_active()`, and
      `clear_namespace()`.
- [ ] Extend the internal API factory with only the prompt-handle validation
      capability proven necessary in Phase 1; preserve existing provider call
      sites and current map-handle semantics.
- [ ] Add the exact provider and API error prefixes from the proposal.
- [ ] Build and validate one versioned facade over one healthy process-wide
      UserPrompt runtime.
- [ ] Ensure every `new()` returns a distinct immutable namespace-bound API and
      repeated construction reuses the runtime without replacing services,
      hooks, presentation owners, or active state.
- [ ] Export `UserPromptServiceProvider` from
      `reqscript('dwarfuicore/services')` alongside the existing providers and
      keep `reqscript('dwarfuicore')` free of service APIs.
- [ ] Implement `cancel`, `is_active`, and `clear_namespace` with exact handle,
      namespace, callback, and return semantics.
- [ ] Prevent same-namespace prompt reentry until `clear_namespace()` callback
      dispatch finishes.
- [ ] Add runtime service cache, initializer/validator, dependency order,
      explicit teardown order, module registry, and package contract entries.
- [ ] Preserve tooltip and context-menu API surfaces and namespace state.

### Phase 6 completion gate

- [ ] Provider tests prove constructor validation, lazy/transactional
      acquisition, distinct immutable APIs, namespace binding, singleton reuse,
      current-generation validation, and non-destructive construction.
- [ ] API tests prove all methods, return values, prefixes, error tokens, handle
      precedence, and namespace cleanup semantics match the proposal.
- [ ] Tests prove tooltip and context-menu providers gain no methods and retain
      healthy process state across UserPrompt construction.
- [ ] The follow-up review compares every public LuaDoc type and operation with
      the approved proposal and current provider conventions.

## Phase 7 - Integrate transactional activation, input, and menu exclusion

- [ ] Implement admission in the proposal's exact order: validate, reject busy,
      resolve a supported map-capable surface, prepare every fallible dependency,
      snapshot the indicator, close any menu, and commit using only non-failing
      assignments.
- [ ] Immediately after the atomic commit, invalidate the selected presentation
      owner so the newly published prompt appears without waiting for unrelated
      game redraw activity. Treat unexpected invalidation failure as a contained
      internal prompt failure while preserving owned-input consumption.
- [ ] Fail absence of a supported current map surface with `INVALID_ARGUMENT`
      before closing a menu or publishing prompt ownership.
- [ ] Roll back preparation failure with no prompt, consumer, render intent,
      tooltip suppression, context-menu mutation, or indicator ownership.
- [ ] Close an existing DwarfUICore context menu during successful activation
      without invoking a selection callback.
- [ ] Add an internal context-menu opening guard covering every opening path,
      not only right-click dispatch, so no menu can open while a prompt is active.
- [ ] Keep context-menu registrations unchanged and remove the guard immediately
      when the prompt terminates.
- [ ] Bind the completed-render owner's authoritative intent and
      ordinary-tooltip suppression flag to the committed active prompt, and
      clear both through the terminal cleanup order.
- [ ] Activate the prompt input consumer exactly while the prompt is pending and
      give it first refusal ahead of context-menu and predecessor handling.
- [ ] `_MOUSE_L_DOWN`: always return true with no sample, terminal transition,
      callback, later-consumer call, or predecessor call.
- [ ] `_MOUSE_L`: always sample `dfhack.gui.getMousePos()` exactly once in that
      handler, copy `{x, y, z}` or nil, consume, complete, clear ownership, and
      dispatch `on_select(position_or_nil)`.
- [ ] Accept `_MOUSE_L` without a prior down event and perform no gesture latch,
      hit test, map ownership/focus check, validity check, or nil rejection.
- [ ] `_MOUSE_R_DOWN`: always consume without terminating.
- [ ] `_MOUSE_R` and `LEAVESCREEN`: always consume, cancel, clear ownership, and
      dispatch `on_cancel()` when supplied without sampling a completion value.
- [ ] For composite input tables, apply cancellation precedence
      (`LEAVESCREEN` or `_MOUSE_R`), then `_MOUSE_L`, then nonterminal down
      boundaries, with exactly one outcome.
- [ ] Delegate wheel, movement, pause, and every other unowned input unchanged.
- [ ] Keep owned events consumed when prompt dispatch or callback processing
      fails; never leak them to context-menu or base-game behavior.
- [ ] Connect the shared dispatcher's protected-failure notification to internal
      prompt cancellation so the owned event remains consumed and the prompt
      cannot remain pending after handler failure.

### Phase 7 completion gate

- [ ] Focused tests prove the complete input table, composite precedence, fresh
      one-sample completion including nil, no latching/validation, exact callback
      dispatch, and unchanged unowned delegation.
- [ ] Tests prove existing menus close on activation, every opening path is
      blocked while active across namespaces, and normal menu behavior resumes
      immediately after termination.
- [ ] Tests prove atomic activation and rollback at every injected fallible
      preparation point, with no failing operation between menu close and commit.
- [ ] Tests prove committed prompt state alone controls authoritative render
      intent and tooltip suppression, activation requests an immediate redraw,
      and both clear on every user-input and explicit-API terminal path integrated
      through this phase. Phase 8 proves the remaining lifecycle paths.
- [ ] The follow-up review searches for conditional left completion, cached
      indicator completion, owned-event delegation, context-menu bypass, and
      pending-but-inert state.

## Phase 8 - Complete lifecycle, failure, and reload integration

- [ ] Implement the fixed terminal order: mark inactive/remove pending state,
      release input, clear prompt render intent and tooltip suppression,
      restore/relinquish indicator, invalidate presentation, then invoke one
      callback behind failure containment.
- [ ] Apply completion only to left release and cancellation to right release,
      Escape, `cancel`, owning `clear_namespace`, input-root loss,
      presentation-root loss, world/map unload, internal prompt failure, and
      explicit Core reload.
- [ ] Invoke `on_cancel()` when supplied for every cancellation cause and never
      invoke `on_select` for the same prompt.
- [ ] Allow completion, right-click, Escape, and explicit-cancel callbacks to
      synchronously open a replacement prompt after old ownership is clear.
- [ ] Reject same-namespace reentry during `clear_namespace()` until cleanup
      callback dispatch completes.
- [ ] Reject reentry during input-root loss, presentation-root loss, world/map
      unload, service failure, and Core retirement through the corresponding
      unavailable surface/runtime state.
- [ ] Mark Core runtime retirement before reload cancellation callbacks and
      retire UserPrompt state before shared input/render owners.
- [ ] Preserve reload-safe inert trampoline adoption for the next generation
      and invalidate every old API and handle.
- [ ] Ensure partial initialization, service disablement, callback failure, and
      teardown failure leave tooltip/context-menu registrations and foreign
      owners intact.
- [ ] Fault-inject every terminal cleanup operation. Prove prompt-owned state is
      cleared or irrevocably relinquished before exact-once callback dispatch,
      no prompt can revive or remain pending, foreign owners remain intact, and
      every contained cleanup failure is diagnosed.
- [ ] Add lifecycle and failure diagnostics without exposing consumer callbacks.

### Phase 8 completion gate

- [ ] Integrated tests cover every lifecycle cause, exact callback choice/count,
      real input/render/tooltip/indicator cleanup order, eligible and rejected
      reentry, callback failure, partial initialization, and old-generation
      invalidation; these replace the Phase 4 stand-ins with actual adapters.
- [ ] Lifecycle tests exercise input-root and presentation-root loss separately,
      including exact-once cancellation, rejected callback reentry, resource
      cleanup, and retained foreign ownership for each.
- [ ] Terminal fault-injection tests cover every cleanup operation and prove the
      cleanup-failure guarantees without weakening callback ordering or count.
- [ ] Reload tests prove no hidden prompt, active consumer, tooltip suppression,
      stale renderer, indicator damage, duplicate hook, or retired healthy
      facade remains.
- [ ] Existing tooltip and context-menu lifecycle/reload suites remain green.
- [ ] The follow-up review verifies the terminal order and all reentry rules
      directly against the proposal's state and sequence diagrams.

## Phase 9 - Complete documentation, packaging, and automated qualification

- [ ] Add public README documentation and examples for provider acquisition,
      options, callbacks, nil completion, handle operations, busy behavior,
      namespace border attribution, input consumption, cancellation, menu
      exclusion, tooltip suppression, indicator behavior, lifecycle, and reload.
- [ ] Update service-provider documentation only where required to list the new
      established-pattern provider and `SERVICE_BUSY`; do not revive deleted
      planning documents.
- [ ] Ensure public docs distinguish the consumer-provided prompt title from the
      automatically bound namespace border label.
- [ ] Update rockspec/package version as required, expanded inventory, archive
      inventory, module registry tests, package contract tests, reload command
      tests, and installation-resolution checks.
- [ ] Add or finish focused unit suites for shared input, shared render,
      prompt values/handles/state, renderer, namespace label, indicator,
      provider/facade, activation, menu exclusion, lifecycle, failure, and reload.
- [ ] Add focused DwarfSpec scenarios for input consumption, menu exclusion,
      tooltip suppression/resumption, prompt rendering, namespace attribution,
      native indicator motion/restore, callbacks, lifecycle, and reload.
- [ ] Run every focused unit suite and record exact totals.
- [ ] Run focused tooltip and context-menu unit/live regressions after the shared
      changes and record them separately from UserPrompt evidence.
- [ ] Run `./tools/Run-UnitTests.ps1` and record complete totals.
- [ ] Run `./tools/Check-UnitTestNaming.ps1`.
- [ ] Run `./tools/Check-LuaSyntax.ps1 -IncludeTests` and record file counts.
- [ ] Run `./tools/Build.ps1 -LiveReload:$false` without making a live claim.
- [ ] Run `./tools/Publish.ps1`, then rerun
      `./tools/VerifyPackage.ps1 -SourceDir src -ZipPath <published-zip>
      -ExpandedPath <published-directory>` against the emitted paths; inspect
      expanded and archive contents separately.
- [ ] Run the complete DwarfSpec suite with a terminal structured result and
      `cleanup_confirmed`.
- [ ] Inspect the built and installed package for all UserPrompt/shared modules,
      exact registry order, one owner per hook seam, and no obsolete file.
- [ ] Audit public symbols, production source, package contents, and the complete
      diff against every proposal non-goal and rejected alternative; record that
      no prohibited prompt operation, lifecycle mechanism, consumer migration,
      UI owner, tooltip exposure, marker path, or completion source was added.
- [ ] Run `git diff --check` after generated-artifact and temporary-state cleanup.

### Phase 9 completion gate

- [ ] Focused and complete automated results are green and recorded separately;
      unit/model assertions are not promoted to live rendered success.
- [ ] Public documentation, LuaDoc, proposal, implementation, registry/reload
      order, expanded package, and archive package agree exactly.
- [ ] The built and installed module resolution identifies the intended package
      and contains no parallel hook owner or stale service name.
- [ ] The follow-up review finds no debug code, unauthorized consumer migration,
      missing package file, deleted-document reference, or unrecorded artifact.

## Phase 10 - Perform live acceptance and independent final review

- [ ] Install the exact verified package in the configured DFHack test
      environment and start a fresh process whenever installed resolution
      changes.
- [ ] Open a prompt with distinctive namespace, title, and message values and
      capture final-screen evidence of all three in their specified locations.
- [ ] Open prompts with embedded newlines and empty title/message combinations;
      capture final-screen evidence that accepted text is preserved and the two
      interior regions remain distinct when non-empty.
- [ ] Prove the full namespace is centered in the top border when it fits and
      the deterministic ASCII truncation rule is used on a constrained viewport.
- [ ] Move the pointer across normal, edge, and unavailable screen positions;
      prove preferred below-pointer placement, clamping, motion, temporary hide,
      artifact-free redraw, and restoration.
- [ ] Prove ordinary tooltip presentation is suppressed while active, its state
      remains registered, and its presentation resumes afterward.
- [ ] Prove `_MOUSE_L_DOWN` is consumed without sampling or base-game behavior
      and leaves the prompt pending.
- [ ] Prove `_MOUSE_L` is consumed, samples once, reports a detached position or
      nil, removes prompt ownership before callback, and does not trigger the
      corresponding base-game click.
- [ ] Prove right down is consumed without termination and right release and
      Escape each consume and cancel exactly once.
- [ ] Open a context menu before activation and prove it closes without
      selection; prove no namespace can open one while active and normal menu
      behavior resumes afterward.
- [ ] Prove representative unowned input delegates unchanged.
- [ ] Capture the native map indicator following hover, hiding off-map, restoring
      prior owned state, and causing no pan, recenter, z-level, or follow-state
      change.
- [ ] In the installed runtime, after UserPrompt's first native indicator write,
      mutate the actual native value as an external owner would; prove the next
      prompt update detects the mismatch, permanently stops writing for that
      prompt, and skips snapshot restoration. Keep simulated fault-injection
      evidence separate as a unit result.
- [ ] Prove completion uses the fresh event sample rather than the last visual
      indicator position.
- [ ] Exercise completion, right-click, Escape, and explicit-cancel callbacks
      that synchronously create a replacement prompt.
- [ ] Exercise namespace cleanup, input-root loss, presentation-root loss,
      world/map unload, internal failure, and explicit Core reload separately;
      inspect service, input, render, tooltip, menu, indicator, hook, API, handle,
      callback count, and rejected reentry state after each.
- [ ] Run a second independent review against every proposal requirement,
      checklist item, diff, test result, rendered artifact, installed-runtime
      observation, and cleanup record.
- [ ] Remove temporary fixtures, installed test state, active prompts, screens,
      hooks, disposable registrations, captures/logs requiring cleanup, and
      generated artifacts that are not deliverables; record `cleanup_confirmed`.

### Phase 10 completion gate

- [ ] Final rendered-screen evidence proves namespace attribution, title/message
      presentation, pointer behavior, tooltip suppression, and native indicator
      behavior; unit layout assertions alone do not.
- [ ] Installed-runtime evidence proves exact input consumption, completion,
      both cancellation gestures, menu exclusion, unowned delegation, callback
      reentry, lifecycle cleanup, reload safety, owner restoration, and actual
      external-indicator-takeover relinquishment.
- [ ] The independent review reports no unmet proposal or checklist item and no
      unexplained divergence.
- [ ] Cleanup is terminal and `cleanup_confirmed` is recorded.

## Definition of done

- [ ] `reqscript('dwarfuicore/services')` exports immutable
      `UserPromptServiceProvider` alongside the unchanged existing providers.
- [ ] `UserPromptServiceProvider:new(1, namespace)` returns a distinct immutable
      namespace-bound API backed by one process-wide UserPrompt runtime.
- [ ] `prompt_map_location(options)` accepts exactly `title`, `message`,
      `on_select`, and optional `on_cancel`, copies all retained values, and
      returns an opaque owned `MapLocationPrompt` handle.
- [ ] The bound API namespace, never consumer prompt options, is the complete
      authoritative owner and border-label source.
- [ ] Only one prompt is active process-wide; concurrent requests fail with
      `SERVICE_BUSY`, and admission cannot leave pending-but-inert state.
- [ ] Left down always consumes without sampling; left release always consumes
      and completes with one fresh copied `{x, y, z}|nil` sample without
      validation or latching.
- [ ] Right down always consumes without terminating; right release and Escape
      always consume and cancel.
- [ ] Prompt-owned input remains consumed on failure; every other input delegates
      according to the preserved predecessor contract.
- [ ] An existing context menu closes on activation, none can open while active,
      registrations remain intact, and normal behavior resumes on termination.
- [ ] The prompt window follows below the pointer where space permits, clamps,
      hides when pointer coordinates are unavailable, remains input-transparent,
      and suppresses only ordinary tooltip presentation.
- [ ] The full owning namespace is centered in the top border when it fits;
      sizing and ASCII truncation follow the proposal, and prompt title/message
      remain distinct interior content.
- [ ] The prompt directly drives `recenter_indicator_m`, hides it off-map,
      respects external takeover, restores prior owned state, and never calls
      `revealInDwarfmodeMap()` or `renderMapOverlay()`.
- [ ] Completion always uses a fresh input-event sample rather than cached
      presentation or indicator state.
- [ ] Terminal state, input, render intent, tooltip suppression, and indicator
      ownership clear in proposal order before exact-once callback dispatch.
- [ ] User-terminal callbacks support replacement prompt reentry; cleanup and
      unavailable-runtime boundaries reject reentry as specified.
- [ ] Handles enforce service, contract-major, namespace, and generation
      ownership with exact error precedence and no public internal diagnostics.
- [ ] Input-root loss, presentation-root loss, world/map unload, failure,
      namespace cleanup, and Core reload each leave no hidden prompt, active
      consumer, suppression, stale renderer, indicator damage, duplicate hook,
      or healthy retired facade.
- [ ] Existing tooltip and context-menu unit, live, render, lifecycle, reload,
      registry, and package contracts remain green.
- [ ] Every new or modified class and method has LuaDoc, every closed
      discriminator is an immutable numeric enum, and every production module
      follows module/loading/registry conventions.
- [ ] Focused unit, complete unit, naming, syntax, offline build, expanded
      package, archive, focused live, complete live, rendered-screen,
      installed-runtime, reload, cleanup, and diff evidence pass as distinct
      gates.
- [ ] All phase follow-up reviews and the final independent review confirm the
      implementation fulfills the approved proposal before this checklist is
      marked complete.

